      ":question/id": "4f4e7729-4448-5202-a479-0d1795c351cb",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=- \\frac{27}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(-3)=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}-(3)=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=-3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{3}{2})-(-3)=- \\frac{27}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=- \\frac{27}{2}-(-3)-(3)=- \\frac{27}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=- \\frac{27}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=-3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{27}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
